  "query_preview": "..\n\nA broad band of showers spans the central Great Lakes and extends \ninto the central Plains this afternoon. It is on schedule to migrate \nacross the rest of SE Mi tonight while gaining intensity along and \nnear the cold front that is already moving into central Lower Mi at \nforecast issuance. The rainband is sharpened by frontogenesis in the \nlow to mid levels as the mid/upper level trough and jet entrance \nregion move eastward into a more complementary position that is \nreflected in model depictions of notable 850-700 mb theta-e gradient \nincrease across the frontal zone. The same theta-e projections show \nthe mature moisture axis also still tapping a hybrid tropical \nPacific/Gulf supply and feeding it into the Great Lakes along and \nahead of the frontal zone. At least a brief period of heavier rain \noccurs in this setup as the 12Z DTX sounding measured PW near 1.8 \ninches and as HREF and REFS mean PW touches 2 inches as the system \nmoves through SE Mi tonight. Otherwise, the steady NW to SE pace of \nthe frontal passage limits flooding potential until the pattern \nexits early Friday morning. \n\nExpansive surface high pressure settling into the northern Plains \ntoday builds aggressively into the Great Lakes during the day \nFriday. It brings an air mass that is cool enough to produce \nscattered to broken cumulus during the afternoon which becomes dry \nenough for a rapid clearing trend Friday evening. Radiational \ncooling then wins out over any lingering light north wind and/or \nthin cirrus during the night. Guidance low temperatures in the mid \nto upper 30s look good north of metro Detroit by Saturday morning.  \n\nThe larger scale westerlies stay progressive during the weekend with \na model consensus on the next low pressure system arriving in \nnorthern Ontario Sunday. The trailing cold front might be capable of \na stray shower as it sweeps through the Great Lakes while high \npressure traps Gulf moisture near the coast to mid Atlantic. The \nfront ushers in a new round of high pressure and below normal \ntemperatures as the weather highlight for early next week.",
